we have an update for you on a six-month  cbs news investigation into a military  base  that was used by u.s service members  after 9 11.  we've shown you how veterans suffered  serious illness after they were exposed  to toxic materials at the base it's in  uzbekistan it's known as k2  in one of his last acts as  commander-in-chief president trump  signed an executive order  that enables aid for those very veterans  senior investigative correspondent  catherine herridge is at the pentagon  for us this morning catherine good  morning to you what does this mean  well tony the executive order does not  link kate to an illness but it does  recognize k2 vets are sick  and should be considered for the same  programs as those who served in iraq and  afghanistan  after years of being dismissed it is a  significant step forward  it was disbelief because it was already  a moon shot  k2 veteran mark jackson says response is  poured in on this facebook page  after an executive order was signed by  president trump  during his final hours in office  hundreds of the members had logged in  and written all sorts of things about  how thankful they were  and after years of resistance k2 vets  reported  the v.a was reaching out to them we even  had a few people  put screen shots of emails they had  gotten from the va  that week jackson is one of at least  2500 vets  who self-report illness including rare  cancers  they believe are linked to a former  soviet air base in uzbekistan  known as k2 after 9 11  u.s forces launched classified missions  from the base into neighboring  afghanistan  we were the first responders to 9 11 so  we got sick  first the investigation documented k2  was a dumping ground  used chemical weapons equipment soil  saturated with jet fuel  radiation and chemical agent warning  signs dotted the base  what does the executive order get the k2  veterans  the executive order demands that the va  and the dod  follow their own rules respect  uzbekistan  as a combat support area jackson told us  it opens the door to previously denied  services  every veteran of uzbekistan can now go  into the va and receive the same care  as someone in afghanistan the executive  order ultimately is a first step  towards this direct link between k2  service  and illness yes everywhere is decay  when we first met jackson he shared his  deployment journal  a raw and unsettling account of his 10  months at k2  every yellow tab is a day where i felt  sick jackson's thyroid stopped  functioning  two years after he left the base and  during the course of our investigation  he received a preliminary cancer  diagnosis  an all too familiar story for k2  veterans  our team profiled former air force  mechanic doug wilson  who spent three months at k2 he can no  longer work or drive  after a rare cancer caused brain damage  wilson's plight  caught the attention of lawmakers mr  wilson rides a mile and a half  in a mechanical wheelchair twice a week  to attend his physical therapy  appointments  in december after seeing wilson's story  then acting defense secretary chris  miller  who as a special forces commander passed  through k2  drafted the executive order let's err on  the side of the veteran  and not get caught up in bureaucracy and  endless studies like we've done in the  past  now the defense department has just one  year to conduct  a rigorous study into the health  consequences of k2  this isn't a 5 10 15 year survey  they have 365 days to reduce results  that is a huge step forward says former  army intelligence analyst elba barr  who blames her reproductive health  issues on toxic exposure at the base  disbelief and then just validation  of a decade's worth of issues k2 vets  are now looking to the biden  administration to finish the job  i would ask the president himself help  us you can fix this  you can get us the presumption of  service connection and get these sick  and dying veterans help that they need  and the families of those who have  already died  the help that they deserve a  spokesperson for defense secretary lloyd  austin tells cbs news he is aware of the  k2 health issues  and he will consult with the va about  the most appropriate way forward adding  the secretary believe strongly in our  nation's obligation  to care for veterans and their families  vlad  such an important story catherine glad  you're staying on top of it thank you  very much
 

